

Exercise: Error Diagnosis Challenge
Scenario Selected: 1. Off by One Error - IndexError

Error Description:
`IndexError: list index out of range` means code tried to access `items[3]` but list only has indexes 0,1,2. Loop `range(len(items) + 1)` runs 4 times for 3 items. Last iteration crashes. Relevant line is line 10 `items[i]`, not line 25.

Root Cause:
Line 8 has `+1` bug:
for i in range(len(items) + 1):  # len=3 -> range 0,1,2,3
Developer confused display number `i+1` with access index `i`. Added +1 to fix print but broke range. Chain: 3 items -> loop 4 times -> i=3 -> crash.

*Suggested Solution:*
Fix by removing +1 and using enumerate:
def print_inventory_report(items):
    print("===== INVENTORY REPORT =====")
    for i, item in enumerate(items):
        print(f"Item {i+1}: {item['name']} - Quantity: {item['quantity']}")
    print("============================")
*Learning Points:*
1. Use `enumerate()` not `range(len())` to avoid off-by-one
2. `range(3)` = 0,1,2 already, don't add +1
3. Keep display `i+1` separate from access `i`
4. Same bug would crash Task Manager if `for i in range(len(tasks)+1)` used
5. Test boundaries: empty list, 1 item, many items

*Reflection:*
- AI was clearer than docs - docs just define IndexError, AI explained why +1 breaks it
- Hard part manually is finding +1 in range, trace points to main() too
- Better error message: check `if i >= len(items): raise ValueError(f"index {i} out of range for {len(items)} items")`
- Yes, AI taught underlying concept: 0-based vs 1-based counting
